CHUNK({"chunk":"3b","module":"m3","topics":[{"id":"m3-lines","title":"BIS line types and line weights","short":"Lines (IS 10714)","blurb":"The IS 10714 line types A to K and where each is used, thick and thin line widths, and which line wins when two coincide.","source":"Lecture 1, slides 7–14; Lecture 2, slides 2–5","tabs":[{"title":"Line types","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every line on a drawing has a meaning. BIS lists them in <b>IS 10714</b>, each with a letter.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m3-line-types\"></figure>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Type</th><th>Main uses</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A</td><td>Continuous thick</td><td>Visible outlines and edges, lines of cuts and section arrows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B</td><td>Continuous thin (straight or curved)</td><td>Dimension, extension, projection and leader lines, hatching, imaginary lines of intersection, short centre lines, outlines of revolved sections</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C</td><td>Continuous thin freehand</td><td>Limits of partial or interrupted views and sections</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D</td><td>Continuous thin with zigzags</td><td>Long-break line</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E</td><td>Dashed thick</td><td>Permissible surface treatment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>Dashed thin</td><td>Hidden outlines and hidden edges</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G</td><td>Chain thin (long dash, dot)</td><td>Centre lines, lines of symmetry, trajectories, pitch circle of gears and holes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>H</td><td>Chain thin, thick at the ends and at changes of direction</td><td>Cutting planes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>J</td><td>Chain thick</td><td>Lines or surfaces to which a special requirement applies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>K</td><td>Chain thin double-dashed</td><td>Outlines of adjacent parts, alternative and extreme positions of movable parts, centroidal lines, initial outlines before forming, parts in front of the cutting plane</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The Lecture 1 slides letter these lines differently (slide 7 calls hidden lines type C/D and the cutting plane type J; slide 12 adds an \"I\"). This table follows Lecture 2 and IS 10714 (letters A–K, no I). In the exam, learn the <b>appearance and use</b>; quote the letter as your teacher taught it.</p></div>"},{"title":"On a part","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-lines-demo\"></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Outline (A)</b>: every edge you can see.</li><li><b>Hidden (F)</b>: the blind hole in the base is behind the front face, so its edges are dashed.</li><li><b>Centre lines (G)</b> cross at the centre of every hole and run a little past the outline.</li><li><b>Extension and dimension lines (B)</b> carry the size; the <b>leader</b> points to the hole and carries the note Ø12.</li></ul>\n<p>Other lines from the slides: a <b>short-break</b> line (thin freehand) ends a partial view; a <b>long-break</b> line (zigzag) shortens a long uniform part; a <b>locus line</b> (chain thin double-dashed) shows the extreme positions of a moving part.</p>"},{"title":"Line weights","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Lines come in two main thicknesses: <b>thick : thin = 2 : 1</b>.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Ratio</th><th>A0</th><th>A1</th><th>A3</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Thick</td><td>1</td><td>1.0</td><td>0.7</td><td>0.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medium</td><td>0.7</td><td>0.7</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thin</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.35</td><td>0.25</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultra-thin</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.18</td><td>0.13</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Widths in mm (Lecture 1, slide 8). With pencils: <b>HB for thick lines, 2H for thin lines</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The slides quote several sets of widths (0.7/0.35, 0.5/0.25, and 0.7/0.5/0.35/0.18). They agree on the rule: visible outlines thick, everything else thin, thick about twice thin. Minimum space between parallel lines: 0.7 mm.</p></div>"},{"title":"Precedence","html":"<p class=\"lead\">When two lines fall in the same place, draw only the more important one.</p>\n<ol><li><b>Visible outline</b> (thick)</li><li><b>Hidden line</b></li><li><b>Cutting plane line</b></li><li><b>Centre line</b></li><li>Dimension, extension, leader lines and hatching</li></ol>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Visible vs hidden</span><b>Draw the visible line</b></div><div><span>Hidden vs centre</span><b>Draw the hidden line</b></div><div><span>Visible vs centre</span><b>Draw the visible line</b></div></div>\n<h4>Good practice</h4>\n<ul><li>All lines sharp and dense; dash and dot spacing uniform.</li><li>Centre lines and lines of symmetry extend slightly beyond the outline.</li><li>Section hatching: thin lines at <b>45°</b>, evenly spaced (about 2–3 mm apart).</li><li>First decide which features are visible, hidden or symmetric, then pick the line.</li></ul>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Line for visible outlines?","back":"A: continuous thick."},{"front":"Line for hidden edges?","back":"Dashed thin (F in IS 10714)."},{"front":"Line for centre lines and symmetry?","back":"Chain thin, long dash and dot (G)."}],"cardCount":10,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m3-lettering-dim","title":"Lettering and dimensioning","short":"Lettering, dimensions","blurb":"Letter sizes for titles, notes and drawing numbers, the elements of a dimension, and the aligned and unidirectional systems.","source":"Lecture 2, slides 6–10","tabs":[{"title":"Lettering","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Lettering is generally done in <b>capital letters</b>. Different sizes are used for different purposes.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Letter height</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Main titles</td><td>6–8 mm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sub-titles</td><td>3–6 mm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notes, dimension figures</td><td>3–5 mm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drawing number in the title block</td><td>10–12 mm</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For lower-case letters of height <em>h</em>, the body takes <b>0.7h</b> and the ascender or descender <b>0.3h</b>.</p>"},{"title":"Elements of a dimension","html":"<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Extension line</span><b>Thin line from the feature, leaving a small gap and running a little past the dimension line</b></div><div><span>Dimension line</span><b>Thin line with arrowheads, parallel to the length it measures</b></div><div><span>Arrowhead</span><b>Ends the dimension line, touching the extension line</b></div><div><span>Dimension figure</span><b>The size, in mm (no unit written)</b></div><div><span>Leader</span><b>Thin line from a note to the feature, ending in an arrowhead</b></div><div><span>Note</span><b>Text such as \"2 HOLES, Ø12\"</b></div></div>\n<p>All of these are thin continuous lines (type B). The figure under <b>Lines on a part</b> shows them on a part.</p>"},{"title":"Aligned vs unidirectional","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-dim-systems\"></figure>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Aligned system</h4><p>Figures are placed <b>above the dimension line</b>, parallel to it, and read from the <b>bottom or the right side</b> of the sheet. Generally recommended.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Unidirectional system</h4><p>All figures are <b>horizontal</b>, read from the bottom of the sheet; the dimension line is <b>broken</b> in the middle to take the figure.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The common slip: in the aligned system a vertical dimension is read from the <b>right</b>, so its figure sits on the <b>left</b> of the line, turned to read upwards. A figure written below a horizontal line, or read from the left, is marked wrong on slide 8.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Letter height for main titles? For notes?","back":"Titles 6–8 mm; notes and dimension figures 3–5 mm."},{"front":"Letter height of the drawing number in the title block?","back":"10–12 mm."},{"front":"Lettering case on engineering drawings?","back":"Generally capital letters."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m3-line-types","src":"m3/pages/l2-02.png","crop":null,"caption":"IS 10714 line types A to K with their main use.","alt":"Ten sample lines: thick, thin, freehand, zigzag, dashed thick and thin, chain thin, cutting plane, chain thick, double-dashed."},{"id":"m3-lines-demo","src":"m3/pages/l2-04.png","crop":null,"caption":"Outline, hidden, centre, extension, dimension and leader lines on one part.","alt":"Front view of a stepped block with a hole, hidden blind hole, centre lines, a 90 dimension and a Ø12 leader."},{"id":"m3-dim-systems","src":"m3/pages/l2-08.png","crop":null,"caption":"The same 50 × 30 plate dimensioned in the aligned and the unidirectional system.","alt":"Two rectangles; left with figures parallel to dimension lines, right with horizontal figures in broken dimension lines."}]});
